# hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh (gf180-sram#94): this repo carries a
# hand-patched local fork of the vendored generic write-confinement guard
# with several gf180-sram-specific fixes that were never accepted upstream
# into Repo Skills (symlink-ancestor-resolution #69/#70, for-loop
# loop-variable binding #63/#66, BSD `sed -i ''` script/target
# disambiguation #49/#77/#79, leading-keyword strip #67/#71, `cd`/redirect
# same-command var resolution #68/#72, and the sim/lib/pdk_env.sh
# mktemp-scratch exemption #64/#65/#82). `resync-installed.sh`'s
# `CANONICAL_GUARD_PRESENT` probe (5 marker greps against
# .claude/skills/repo/hooks/guard-destructive.sh) never evaluates to 1 for
# this repo's installed canonical guard version (it is missing the
# `gh-comment-body-literal-at` marker), so every resync run considered the
# vendored copy eligible for wholesale replacement and silently reverted
# every one of the fixes above -- four times over (#78, #80/#81/#83/#84/#85,
# #88, #92), each restored once and wiped again by the very next resync
# commit. Pinning this path here is the structural fix: the file is a
# deliberate, permanent local divergence from the vendored default (Repo
# Skills' documented posture (a), keep a git-tracked local copy), so any
# genuine future upstream improvement to the vendored guard must be
# reviewed and merged into this file by hand rather than arriving silently
# via resync.
